course: dictionary
lesson: 6691
title: Words that come now and then
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Occasional is an adjective that tells you how often something happens. It means happening sometimes, but not often or regularly. «Crisps and sweets have little goodness and lots of salt, sugar and fat, so treats are kept small and occasional.» Treats are not eaten every day, so occasional must mean sometimes.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. Kept small and the idea of treats both tell you that occasional treats are not a daily habit. Try a swap: «treats are kept small and sometimes» still makes sense, so occasional means sometimes.
explain: In a weather forecast, «occasional showers» means rain now and then, not rain all day. Occasional can also describe something made for a special event, like occasional music. Check the words around it each time to see which meaning fits.
fact: Occasional means happening sometimes, but not often or regularly.
fact: «Occasional» can also describe something made for a special event, like occasional music.
fact: «Occasional showers» in a weather forecast means rain now and then.
fact: An occasional treat is a sweet you have only sometimes.
fact: Phrases to know: «the occasional…», «occasional showers», and «occasional table» (a small side table).
exercise: «Crisps and sweets have little goodness and lots of salt, sugar and fat, so treats are kept small and occasional.» What does occasional mean here? | Eaten every single day | *Happening sometimes, not often | Sour and bitter | Very big and filling
exercise: The forecast says «occasional showers» for today. What should you expect? | Snow only at night | No clouds at all | *Rain now and then | Rain all day without a break
exercise: Which word is closest in meaning to occasional? | Loudly | Always | *Sometimes | Never
test: Ella says an occasional treat is a sweet she eats every morning. Is she right? | *No, an occasional treat is only eaten now and then | Yes, because occasional means daily | Yes, every morning is the best time for it | No, it is a sweet that is never eaten
test: Which sentence uses occasional correctly? | The shop is occasional and open every hour of the day. | The bus is occasional, so it runs every single minute. | My dog is occasional and barks all night long. | *We go to the cinema on occasional Saturdays, not every week.
exam: Which best describes an occasional visitor to a house? | *Someone who comes now and then, not often | Someone who lives there every day | Someone who is never allowed inside | Someone who is always loud
